Nehrim

Oblivion conversion mod - some Oblivion mods may be applicable, check first.
Engine is notorious for crashing, see about setting up autosaves using full saves (assume same rules as modded Skyrim apply - no quicksaves, only full, no overwriting old saves, ideally no reloading)

Oblivion should support 21:9 resolution, though with harmless glitches in loading screens (which are patched)

some people claim that proton9 works better for Oblivion.

ENBoost is not to be used with Linux - instead look into things that DXVK and wine do. https://github.com/rockerbacon/modorganizer2-linux-installer/issues/177
	Ensure you set this in the game launch options, so it is large address aware: WINE_FORCE_LARGE_ADDRESS_AWARE=1 %command%
	Configure dxvk.conf with d3d9.evictManagedOnUnlock

It is said that codecs packs should be removed - caused freezes every minute for someone
It is said that "Memory Purge" should be turned off, caused crashes when loading new areas

------------
Installation
------------
Run NehrimSetup.sh
It installs nehrim, just let the default options run. Do not launch when done.
Next it installs Wrye Bash, let it run, you will need to install it to another location manually. Install it to the "GOG Games" folder. Uncheck the checkbox to view the notes, and do not launch it.
Next it installs LOOT - install just for that user, and then let it look for the newest lists online. Uncheck the button to launch it out of the gate.
Next it opens the bsa unpacker - go to the wineprefix's c drive\gog games\nehrim\data and unpack "N - Sounds.bsa to the Nehrim installation folder. Then close the bsa unpacker. This fixes the sounds not playing. 
Folder structure -- ******/drive_c/GOG Games/Nehrim at Fate's Edge GoG/Data/sound/fx/(all manner of sound effect folders)

------------------------------
Tes4 edit - for cleaning mods
------------------------------
Open the TES4edit archive, and copy 'Edit Scripts', 'TES4Edit.exe', and 'TES4EditQuickAutoClean.exe' to the Nehrim Data folder
Now go to MO2, and add executables for Tes4Edit and Tes4EditQAC, connect them to the proper .exe files
If TES4Edit is not loading the plugins in MO2, configure it's executable options to start in the main Nehrim folder. 
If TES4Edit cannot find the .ini, create the 'My Games/Oblivion' folder in your wine c_drive docuemtns folder, and copy the oblivion.ini file from your Nehrim folder over

Install all regular plugins, etc. Sort load order
[landscape LOD textures - needs manual install, refer to Readme]
[Easiest to install grass fix manually as well]

-----------------
What is this for?
-----------------
Setting dxvk.conf (replace enboost) Take the dxvk.conf file, and place it in the Main game directory (where all the exe's are). Add this line to it: d3d9.evictManagedOnUnlock = True

---------------------------------
Be careful with BethINI on Nehrim
---------------------------------
Unpack BethINI somewhere in the c_drive
BethINI: WINEPREFIX=/home/$USER/Games/Nehrim-wineGE wine /location/of/BethINI.exe
Select default,(let it load), Ultra (let it load), recomended tweaks (let it load), then save and exit

Set fDlgFocus to 15 in oblivion.ini - disables the dialog zoom
Autosaves should be off when you disable the three settings in the oblivion.ini

On game startup:
	click the menu that asks if you want to go back to default Nehrim settings - choose no.
	disable autosaves (Simple Saves Plus replaces autosave - implements an automatic regular save feature)
	configure Simple Saves Plus


(maybe try ENB in the future)
ENB may still work on Linux, check and see
(it sounds like underwater effects should be off with enb - or else they cause a circle of blur on the ground by the player)
